 "productionNotes": "No production total is asserted for the generation. The only model-by-model figures retrieved are a web transcription of the Standard Catalog of American Light-Duty Trucks, 3rd edition, published by Out In The Shop; the book itself was not consulted, and the transcription has visible defects. It gives the 1970 short-bed Fleetside and short-bed Step-Side the identical figure of 40,754, which looks like a copied cell, and leaves the 1968 K10 long-bed Fleetside blank. Summing six years across those gaps would give a figure with more precision than it has earned.\n\nWhat the tables do show is shape. Adding the C10 Fleetside and Step-Side pickup lines year by year gives roughly 275,000 for 1967, 316,000 for 1968, 390,000 for 1969, 328,000 for 1970, 265,000 for 1971 and 343,000 for 1972, consistent with Hagerty's statements that 1969 was the biggest year, that 1967 and 1971 were the smallest, and that more than two million half-tons were built. The long-bed Fleetside dominated: 268,233 in 1969 and 273,249 in 1972, against 39,730 short-bed Fleetsides in 1972. The long-bed Step-Side fell from 19,969 in 1967 to 7,269 in 1971. Four-wheel drive was a sideline, with 1,046 K10 short-bed Fleetsides built for 1967 and 6,069 for 1972.\n\nPrices need the same caution. The transcribed tables list a 1967 short-bed Fleetside at $2,371 and a 1972 at $2,680, but Indie Auto, citing the book's second edition, gives the 1968 short Fleetside at $2,317 and 3,333 lb where the third-edition transcription shows $2,468 and 3,467 lb, and HotCars gives an unexplained 1967 MSRP of $2,221. One owner quoted by Hagerty recalls paying $2,700 for a loaded 1971 Cheyenne Super, below the $2,816 catalog base for a 1971 short Fleetside; the sources consulted cannot reconcile the two.",
